<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>@CE_APP_OUTPUT_NAME@</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.celestialengine.app</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Celestial Engine</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>@PROJECT_VERSION@</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>
	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>AppIcon</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>10.15</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticGraphicsSwitching</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
	<string>NSApplication</string>

	<!--
		Game Mode (macOS 14+). Launch Services decides eligibility from the application category,
		so a game that does not declare itself one never qualifies, whatever else it sets.
		LSSupportsGameMode is the explicit opt-in; GCSupportsGameMode is its Game Controller
		counterpart, which is what lowers Bluetooth latency for controllers and audio.
		Note that macOS only ACTIVATES Game Mode while the app is in full screen - windowed, an
		otherwise perfectly eligible game never enters it.
	-->
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.games</string>
	<key>LSSupportsGameMode</key>
	<true/>
	<key>GCSupportsGameMode</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
		Metal developer tooling, substituted per configuration - present in Debug and Release,
		absent in Dist. See CE_APP_METAL_TOOLING_KEYS in App/CMakeLists.txt.

		What MetalHudEnabled actually does, measured on this SDK rather than assumed: it makes Metal
		map libMTLHud.dylib into the process at startup, idle. MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 is what draws the
		HUD, and it draws it with or without this key - the key is an opt-in, not a prerequisite.
		Its value is keeping the HUD one environment variable away on launch paths where setting one
		is awkward (Finder, an IDE run configuration). Dist does without it exactly so that a shipped
		build never maps that dylib at all.

		MetalCaptureEnabled is the same kind of opt-in for programmatic GPU frame capture through
		MTLCaptureManager, whose environment counterpart is MTL_CAPTURE_ENABLED=1. Capture refuses to
		start unless one of the two is set; with it, a capture can be written to an outputURL you
		choose instead of living inside Xcode.
	-->
@CE_APP_METAL_TOOLING_KEYS@
</dict>
</plist>
